I looked at the frontier incantation (and btw just having frontier available is a big help for xcode work). What I found is some seriously odd kant behavior. Here is the bit of frontier I was examining:
    frStation3 code={
        fixFrontier3Here = 0;
        cerr "ANCHOR frontier station 3 -- mint twin via copyOf":;
        frTwin <- copyOf(Grokking["Braced"]);
        frOk = 0;
        **frTwin;
        if frTwin;          frOk = 1;
        dumpContents(frStation3);
        **frTwin;
        if frOk == 1;            cerr "STATION 3 mint twin: PASS -- twin taG reads " frTwin.taG:;
        if frOk == 0;
            cerr "STATION 3 mint twin: FAIL -- copyOf returned nothing":;
            frDead = 1;
        };
At the first invocation of **frTwin frTwin comes thru as the Braced copy. In the second invocation the if statement changes frTwin to be ExpressioN. This is the trace printed when the second **frTwin runs:
frStation3           attribute  length 13	frStation3=frStation3data type has no toString() method other
	CodE                 attribute  noPrint	CodE  other
	this                 attribute  is local noPrint frStation3	this  group
	tempField            attribute  is local noPrint	no data
	fixFrontier3Here     attribute  is local	fixFrontier3Here=0 int
	frTwin               attribute  Braced	frTwin=ExpressioN group
	copyOf               attribute 	no data
	Grokking             attribute  length 79	Grokking=Grokking other
	frOk                 attribute 	frOk=1 int
	dumpContents         attribute 	no data
	frStation3           attribute  length 13	frStation3=frStation3data type has no toString() method other
	taG                  attribute 	taG=1 int
	frDead               attribute 	frDead=0 int
	BlocK                attribute  noPrint length 12	BlocK  string
STATION 3 mint twin: PASS -- twin taG reads  Braced 
You can see the group in frTwin got changed by the if statement into ExpressioN and frOk == 1. The if expression sees Braced has an action: aCTionBraced and runs it (not intended but it happens). aCTionBraced clears Braced so no more groupList and sets Braced.group to ExpressioN. The PASS cerr line prints out frTwin.taG as Braced because opDot() sees frTwin as Braced (runOp only unwraps frTwin once).
Taking out the if statement from frStation3 and setting frOk = 1; gets us thru stations 3 and 4 because Braced then still has its groupList. However, station 5 fails because when I dumpContents for Braced. Here is the station 5 code with my debug probes.
    frStation5 code={
        fixFrontier5Here = 0;
        cerr "ANCHOR frontier station 5 -- compile on twin":;
        frTwin :. isCodeD;
        dumpContents(frTwin);
        **frTwin;
        frOut := compile(frTwin);
        frOk = 0;
        if frOut;           frOk = 1;
        if frOk == 1;            cerr "STATION 5 compile on twin: PASS -- compile returned a value":;
        if frOk == 0;
            cerr "STATION 5 compile on twin: FAIL -- compile refused or the parse failed; texture is above this line":;
            frDead = 1;
        };
    Here is what dumpContent prints out
Braced               attribute  length 5	no data
	leftBrace            attribute 	leftBrace=[ string
	ExpressioN           attribute  length 1	no data
	rightBrace           attribute 	rightBrace=] string
	StorE                attribute  noPrint	StorE  string
	StorE                attribute  noPrint	StorE  string
Note there are two StorE entries on its list and NO CodE entry so the compile craps out.

Bottom line: the if statement in station 3 should not see aCTionBraced as a method (it should be stashed in actionMethod, however, Braced has no rStuff. We currently do not remove rule actions from method when we stash them in actionMethod. I have no idea why Braced has no rStuff.)
    The two StorEs and no CodE need fixing and if fixed maybe station 5 works???
